Anterior Cruciate Ligament (ACL) Injury
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Audible pop during pivot, Rapid hemarthrosis, Positive Lachman test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-007
Reason for Visit: Acute knee trauma with massive, rapid swelling and inability to weight-bear.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and flatmate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage established a high-velocity twisting knee injury accompanied by an audible pop and immediate tense hemarthrosis. Urgent clinical evaluation was mandated to exclude limb-threatening vascular injury, acute tibial plateau fracture, and to implement immediate joint immobilization and potent analgesia to prevent further intra-articular damage prior to morning services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My knee buckled with a loud snap while I was playing netball, and now it's massively swollen and agonizing to move."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute right knee injury sustained 4 hours ago. Timeline: Patient was cutting to change direction when they felt an audible pop and sudden giving way of the knee. Play was immediately ceased due to intense localized pain. Over the subsequent 2-6 hours, the patient developed a rapid, tense joint effusion (hemarthrosis). Currently reports a profound feeling of instability and a complete inability to bear weight.
Functional Status: Immobile, requires assistance of flatmate to transfer.
Social/Safety Concerns: Fall risk due to profound mechanical instability of the primary weight-bearing joint.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/84 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, in visible distress, holding the right knee in 30 degrees of flexion.
Physical Exam: Right knee demonstrates a tense, massive effusion (hemarthrosis) obliterating normal anatomical contours. Skin is intact with no erythema. Deep joint line tenderness present globally. Distal pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) are strong and symmetric; capillary refill <2 seconds. Sensation to light touch intact in all distal nerve distributions. Positive Lachman test demonstrating excessive anterior translation of the tibia with a soft, indistinct endpoint. Anterior drawer test at 90 degrees flexion is positive. Pivot-shift test deferred due to extreme guarding and pain. Medial and lateral collateral ligaments intact to varus/valgus stress.
Environmental Observations: Stairs present at the residence; patient cannot safely navigate them.
Medication Reconciliation: Took 1g Paracetamol 2 hours ago with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Anterior Cruciate Ligament (ACL) Rupture with Hemarthrosis - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Tibial Plateau Fracture - Ruled out clinically by the absence of exquisite, focal bony tenderness directly over the proximal tibial rim, though formal imaging is required.

Patellar Dislocation - Ruled out by central tracking of the patella, normal alignment, and negative patellar apprehension test.

Popliteal Artery Injury - Ruled out by symmetric, bounding distal pulses, brisk capillary refill, and normal distal temperature/color.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient sustained a classic pivoting injury resulting in an acute ACL rupture, evidenced by the audible pop, rapid-onset hemarthrosis, and a positive Lachman test indicating gross anterior instability. The resulting tense effusion and severe mechanical instability rendered the patient completely unable to weight-bear, posing a significant fall risk. Delaying medical assessment risked missing an occult vascular injury or associated fracture, and would have left the patient without adequate immobilization and analgesia for a highly painful acute intra-articular bleed. Urgent after-hours assessment safely ruled out neurovascular compromise, provided necessary structural support via bracing, and instituted appropriate acute pain management protocols.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Ibuprofen 400mg TDS and Codeine 30mg QID PRN for 3 days to manage acute distension pain.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Strictly apply an ice pack wrapped in a towel for 20 minutes every 2 hours. Consequence: Constriction of bleeding intra-articular vessels, leading to a halt in swelling and a reduction in the severe throbbing pain (negative reinforcement).
Action: Keep the knee rigidly locked in the provided splint during all transfers. Consequence: Prevention of the tibia sliding forward, protecting the joint from sudden buckling and agonizing pain spikes (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted a rigid knee immobilizer splint and provided axillary crutches (non-weight-bearing status).
Follow-up: Urgent referral for MRI right knee and review by orthopedic surgery or sports physician within 7 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the foot becomes cold, pale, or numb, or if the calf develops severe, localized swelling and tenderness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; definitive confirmation of ligamentous rupture and associated meniscal/chondral damage requires an MRI. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute swelling and pain will slowly subside over 1-2 weeks. The knee will remain mechanically unstable, requiring surgical reconstruction or extensive physical therapy depending on future specialist consultation.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if you lose feeling in your toes, if your foot turns cold or blue, or if you develop new severe pain in your calf muscle.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The rapid swelling is caused by bleeding from the torn ligament into the closed joint space. The rigid splint and crutches are mandatory to stop the bones from shifting, which would cause further bleeding and cartilage damage.
Actionable Steps: Keep the leg elevated above your heart to drain the fluid. Use the crutches strictly; do not attempt to walk on this leg, as the structural support of the knee is completely gone.

